2. PATHS AND DISTRIBUTIONS

Nobody knows where a stock will be in the future. To reason about it,
we describe the future in two ways.

A path is one possible route the price could take from today to some
future date. Each path is one possible future.

A distribution describes the spread of where the price could be on one
particular date, and how likely each level is.

These are different pieces of information. A distribution tells you
where the price might be on a given date. It says nothing about how it
got there. That difference drives the rest of these notes.

A model of the market is a rule for generating paths. Simulating
thousands of them to price a product is called Monte Carlo simulation.
